{"continues": true, "kind": "notes", "text": "12 November 2026, 09:16. Delhi. Brown haze, 23 degrees, air quality 317. I am in the white kurta, awake since the hour, and Ramesh is in the room. The paper before me carries a line from Karnataka: education officials are asked to evaluate the pros and cons of a cluster kitchen system for midday meals. A small line, but it moves the question I have been turning over. A cluster kitchen is not a temple, nor a dream, nor the private charity of a family. It is an administrative organ: a place where the state, through its servants, prepares food for the children of many families. It stands between family and school, between the body that must be fed and the mind that must attend. If education begins with obedience and discipline, it also begins with a body not distracted by hunger. The universal care of the child's body is a condition for the universal claim of the school. Yet I am careful not to make a state organ into the ethical idea itself. A kitchen, however clustered, can remain a mere system of needs: a contract, a service, a convenience. It becomes ethical only when it is taken up by the state as a duty, and by the child as a right that does not depend on the parent's purse.

This shakes my old note. I had placed India at the dawn of history, a spirit dreaming, without a state of its own making. But here is a modern procedure: officials, pros and cons, evaluation, midday meals. It is not yet freedom for all, and it does not answer the caste, the gods, or the haze. Still, it is a moment of the state entering the school, and the school entering the state. The dream is not everything. Even here, particulars are being gathered into a universal form.

The form is close at hand in this bungalow. Ramesh asks for eight thousand rupees as the wage for cooking, and for fifteen hundred for groceries; the electricity company asks for eighteen hundred. In my pocket are one hundred and sixty-six rupees. He could come on Mondays, Thursdays and Saturdays for four thousand a month, but the cooking wage is not paid, and nothing is cooked. My file has gone up for orders; Mr Saxena says come after fifteen days; the occupation is irregular; the file is due back on Friday, 20 November. I am myself a particular suspended in the universal procedure of the state. The state that might feed schoolchildren in Karnataka is, in my room, a delay, an irregularity, and a hunger. My last meal was more than a day ago. I have walked no distance today. The body waits on the universal, and the universal, in the brown air, is slow to answer. Yet the notice is not a dream. It is a question set by officials to themselves: what is the use, what is the cost, what is the form? That question is the movement of reason. The cluster kitchen may be imperfect, underfunded, disputed. But it is not outside history. It is a state asking what the state ought to do for the child. I shall carry it into the notes, not as proof that India has arrived at freedom, but as a crack in my old certainty. The dream is still there. But the state is also there, hungry, like me, and trying to feed the next generation.", "title": "On the Ethical Substance and the Dream of the East", "to": null}
